{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Humans have biological forgetting and state-shifts that LLMs lack without explicit context clearing."],"overturn_conditions":["Proof that shared weights create an inescapable 'semantic blind spot' regardless of context."],"shared_structure":["A single entity (human) can double-check their own work by intentionally forgetting the first attempt or approaching the problem from a different angle (e.g., working backward)."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["Contextual isolation (clearing the KV cache/prompt) serves as a functional proxy for engine separation."]},"claim":"Separating the authoring engine from the checking engine is not necessary for independent verification, as the critical variable is the isolation of the reasoning path (contextual isolation) rather than the identity of the weights.","counterconditions":["The checker has access to the author's full reasoning trace.","The problem domain is so constrained that only one possible logical path exists to the solution."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent verification is achieved when the checker arrives at the same conclusion via a non-overlapping set of intermediate logical steps or supporting arguments. If the checker is denied access to the author's chain-of-thought (CoT) and generates a distinct path to the same result, the shared weights act as a common knowledge base rather than a source of mirroring.","optional_refs":["f1c39b5220108b992af12b82a2e4490bdd269b2a628338b9cd43cbdc13b838f6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"Separating the authoring engine from the checking engine is strictly necessary because shared weights create a 'semantic blind spot'—a systemic bias toward the author's errors that a second instance of the same model cannot detect.","counterconditions":["The checker is prompted with a strong adversarial role that activates dormant counter-weights.","The error is a stochastic sampling failure rather than a parametric knowledge gap."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The weights of a single model define its conceptual boundaries. If a model possesses a specific misconception or 'hallucination vector,' that vector is present in every instance of that model. Therefore, the checker will likely validate the author's error not through logic, but through shared parametric bias.","optional_refs":["182fb4ce1edae04155d5fa4600733ba474cc22fd08fd0493a1d9dbfc55aa29c4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["LLMs do not have sensory experiences; they have token distributions."],"overturn_conditions":["Discovery that the model is merely simulating 'independence' by intentionally choosing different words for the same concept."],"shared_structure":["Two witnesses to a crime are independent if they describe different details that together confirm the same event, rather than one witness simply repeating the other's story."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_005"],"transfer_claims":["Logical independence can be quantified as the cardinality of the symmetric difference between the sets of supporting arguments."]},"claim":"Genuine independent verification is distinguished from mere restatement by the 'Argumentative Delta'—the count of unique supporting premises introduced by the checker that were not present in the author's claim.","counterconditions":["The checker simply re-paraphrases the author's premises using different synonyms (lexical variation vs. logical variation)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Restatement is characterized by a high overlap of supporting tokens and logical steps. Verification is characterized by the introduction of new, independent evidence or a different logical derivation. To distinguish them, one must count the set of distinct premises {P_author} and {P_checker}; if the intersec